# AUR packages — the half that is not in Arch's own repos.
#
# Same format and the same "not verified against a live mirror" caveat as
# arch/pkglist; see that file's header. install-linux.sh installs these with the
# AUR helper (paru by default) and skips the section entirely if none is found,
# so a machine without an AUR helper still gets everything in pkglist.
#
# Kept separate from pkglist rather than merged and handed wholesale to paru:
# an AUR package builds from source and can prompt, so it is worth seeing which
# of the two lists a failure came from.

# The GitHub CLI's own package is in the repos (as github-cli, in pkglist);
# these are not.
mist-cli
firebase-tools

# mycli and carapace-bin have no official-repo package; both build from
# upstream release binaries via AUR PKGBUILDs.
mycli
carapace-bin

# Homebrew leaves that had no Linux counterpart in either list until
# 2026-09-16, when the two were diffed. Names checked against the AUR RPC that
# day, which is more than the rest of this file can say — see the header.
#   mdcat        — not in extra despite what its age suggests; AUR only.
#   ccat         — the Homebrew `ccat` (colorized cat).
#   ox-bin       — the Homebrew `ox` editor. `ox` (source build) also exists
#                  and lags several minor versions behind; -bin tracks upstream.
#   assh-bin     — the Homebrew `assh`. Same story: `assh` in the AUR is stuck
#                  on 2.3 while -bin is on the current 2.17 line.
#   php-cs-fixer — flagged out-of-date on the AUR as of that check (3.88 vs.
#                  Homebrew's 3.95). Installs, just behind; `composer global
#                  require friendsofphp/php-cs-fixer` is the fallback if the
#                  PKGBUILD stops building.
mdcat
ccat
ox-bin
assh-bin
php-cs-fixer

# iris — deliberately NOT built on Linux. It was in this list as an unverified
# AUR PKGBUILD; the build hung/failed in practice, so it's dropped rather than
# left to stall install-linux.sh's AUR step. .zshrc degrades cleanly without
# it — the `zcache iris` call is a no-op when the binary is absent, so the
# shell comes up without the overlay rather than erroring. Do not re-add it
# without a working Linux build verified first; do not paper over it with a
# `curl | sh` installer, which is what the Brewfile comment rejected for the
# same tool on macOS.
